s":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":3,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"3","text":"Harlow v. Fitzgerald, 457 U.S. 800 (1982)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1982 - Harlow v. Fitzgerald, 457 U.S. 800","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":26,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-3-1-1-60b5da6a","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":3,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"3","text":"Spalding v. Vilas, 161 U.S. 483 (1896)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1896 - Spalding v. Vilas, 161 U.S. 483","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":27,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-3-1-1-4a6c9e45","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":3,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"3","text":"Bradley v. Fisher, 80 U.S. (13 Wall.) 335 (1872)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1871 - Bradley v. Fisher, 80 U.S. (13 Wall.) 335","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":28,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-3-1-1-dc9c2f3b","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":3,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"3","text":"Randall v. Brigham, 74 U.S. (7 Wall.) 523 (1868)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1868 - Randall v. Brigham, 74 U.S. (7 Wall.) 523","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":29,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-3-1-1-5f4bf901","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":3,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"Floyd v. Barker, 12 Co. Rep. 23, 77 Eng. Rep. 1305 (Star Chamber 1607)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1607 - Floyd v. Barker, 77 Eng. Rep. 1305","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":30,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-0ea353bd","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"Kendall v. Stokes, 44 U.S. (3 How.) 87 n.","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1845 - Kendall v. Stokes, 44 U.S. 87","target_page":"87","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"44 U.S. (3 How.) 87 n.","status":"verified","target_quote":"The reader is referred to a former case … these parties, reported in 12 Peters, 524. The decision of the court in the present case is so intimately … with the facts … both, that it is impossible to give a clear account … of the principles established, without a reference to those facts","line":31,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-2001c3e6","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":1,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"Yates v. Lansing, 9 Johns. 395, 431–33 (N.Y. 1811)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1811 - Yates v. Lansing, 9 Johns. 395","target_page":"431","target_page_end":"433","target_pin":"9 Johns. at 431-33","status":"verified","target_quote":"Where a judge acts within his jurisdiction, it would entirely destroy his independence and his usefulness, if he were liable to answer to individuals who might conceive themselves aggrieved by his decisions. It is the lot of humanity to err, and what man would take an office, which would expose him, in the execution of … its duties, to the prosecutions of unfortunate or dissatisfied … No judge would be able to stand up against the expense and … vexation that would result from this position; and it is no less than impolitic, to expose him to amenability for errors, … to which we are more or less subject. This is the true principle … true reason, why judges, acting as judges, that is, acting within … the sphere of their … delegated authority, are protected in England … This is the authority principally relied on, and it will be at once perceived on how slight a foundation. It was a mere obiter opinion, not applicable to the case before the court; but if critically examined, it is susceptible of a construction, not incompatible with truth. The maxim of Ch. J. De Grey is, that in order to extend protection to the judge giving an erroneous judgment, he must be acting as … judge. Now can a … man be said to act as a judge, when he has no … the mere forms or symbols of office, the mere occupation of a judicial bench, constitute a … Unless it can be supposed that the superior courts in England and this country, have jurisdiction coextensive with every object of judicial cognisance, then we must admit that their jurisdiction is not unlimited, and that, consequently, they may act beyond it, and ought to answer for it. Their jurisdiction is unlimited as to place, but not as to the quantity of judicial power. The process of chancery and the supreme court, runs into every county of the state; but their authority does not reach every mode of action, every source of litigation. And, therefore, … to say that those courts shall be protected in all cases, whether they act within their jurisdiction or not, and that inferior courts shall only be shielded when they act within their jurisdiction, is establishing a difference without a reason, and is investing the higher courts with arbitrary and discretionary power, over the lives, liberties and property of our citizens","line":32,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-76002678","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":37,"target_pdf_page_end":39,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"STAC 8/203/38","n":"1","kind":"ecf","target_doc":"DDC-073-01","target_page":"2","target_page_end":"11","target_pin":"App. 1, Ex. FL-1 (the ten membranes)","status":"mapped","target_quote":"","line":33,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-0be886a3","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":2,"target_pdf_page_end":11,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"STAC 8/203/38","n":"1","kind":"ecf","target_doc":"DDC-073-01","target_page":"13","target_page_end":"35","target_pin":"App. 1, Ex. FL-3 (the transcription)","status":"mapped","target_quote":"","line":34,"k":2,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-2-0be886a3","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":13,"target_pdf_page_end":35,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"Exhibit 1","n":"1","kind":"ecf","target_doc":"DDC-072-01","target_page":"1","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"ECF 72-1","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":35,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-54eb0a85","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":1,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"4","text":"Rule 12","n":"1","kind":"rule","target_doc":"Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (Dec. 1, 2025, uscourts.gov official pamphlet)","target_page":"20","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"Rule 12","status":"verified","target_quote":"Every defense to a claim for relief in any pleading must be asserted in the responsive pleading if one is required. But a party may assert the following defenses by motion: (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; (2) lack of personal jurisdiction; (3) improper venue; (4) insufficient process; (5) insufficient service of process; (6) failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted; and (7) failure to join a party under Rule 19. A motion asserting any of these defenses must be made before pleading if a responsive pleading is allowed. If a pleading sets out a claim for relief that does not require a responsive pleading, an opposing party may assert at trial any defense to that claim. No defense or objection is waived by joining it with one or more other defenses or objections in a responsive pleading or in a motion.","line":36,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-4-1-1-09e4f75f","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":4,"target_pdf_page":39,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"5","text":"Webster v. Fall, 266 U.S. 507, 511 (1925)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1925 - Webster v. Fall, 266 U.S. 507","target_page":"511","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"at 511","status":"verified","target_quote":"Counsel for appellant directs our attention to other cases, where this Court proceeded to determine the merits notwithstanding the suits were brought against inferior or subordinate officials without joining the superior. We do not stop to inquire whether all or any of them can be differentiated from the case now under consideration, since in none of them was the point here at issue sug-gested or decided. The most that can be said is that the point was in the cases if anyone had seen fit to raise it. Questions which merely lurk in the record, neither brought to the attention of the court nor ruled upon, are not to be considered as having been so decided as to constitute precedents","line":37,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-5-1-1-f99b223c","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":5,"target_pdf_page":5,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"5","text":"Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. (1 Cranch) 137, 177 (1803)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"1803 - Marbury v. Madison, 5 U.S. (1 Cranch) 137","target_page":"177","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"at 177","status":"verified","target_quote":"It is emphatically the province and duty of the judicial department to … what the law is … who apply the rule to particular … expound and interpret that rule. If two laws … operation of each","line":38,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-5-1-1-7ae9aabe","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":5,"target_pdf_page":41,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"5","text":"Rule 12","n":"1","kind":"rule","target_doc":"Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (Dec. 1, 2025, uscourts.gov official pamphlet)","target_page":"20","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"Rule 12","status":"verified","target_quote":"Every defense to a claim for relief in any pleading must be asserted in the responsive pleading if one is required. But a party may assert the following defenses by motion: (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; (2) lack of personal jurisdiction; (3) improper venue; (4) insufficient process; (5) insufficient service of process; (6) failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted; and (7) failure to join a party under Rule 19. A motion asserting any of these defenses must be made before pleading if a responsive pleading is allowed. If a pleading sets out a claim for relief that does not require a responsive pleading, an opposing party may assert at trial any defense to that claim. No defense or objection is waived by joining it with one or more other defenses or objections in a responsive pleading or in a motion.","line":39,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-5-1-1-09e4f75f","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":5,"target_pdf_page":39,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"5","text":"supra § III","n":"1","kind":"internal","target_doc":"DDC-072","target_page":"2","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"§ III","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":40,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-5-1-1-cdd97b97","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":5,"target_pdf_page":2,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"6","text":"Rule 12(b)(1)","n":"1","kind":"rule","target_doc":"Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (Dec. 1, 2025, uscourts.gov official pamphlet)","target_page":"20","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"Rule 12(b)(1)","status":"verified","target_quote":"But a party may assert the following defenses by motion: ... (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction;","line":41,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-6-1-1-228fc9a2","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":6,"target_pdf_page":39,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"6","text":"Vico Products Co. v. NLRB","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"2003 - Vico Products Co. v. NLRB, 333 F.3d 198","target_page":"","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":42,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-6-1-1-49b5c5bc","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":6,"target_pdf_page":null,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":null},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"6","text":"Rule 12","n":"2","kind":"rule","target_doc":"Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (Dec. 1, 2025, uscourts.gov official pamphlet)","target_page":"20","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"Rule 12","status":"verified","target_quote":"Every defense to a claim for relief in any pleading must be asserted in the responsive pleading if one is required. But a party may assert the following defenses by motion: (1) lack of subject-matter jurisdiction; (2) lack of personal jurisdiction; (3) improper venue; (4) insufficient process; (5) insufficient service of process; (6) failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted; and (7) failure to join a party under Rule 19. A motion asserting any of these defenses must be made before pleading if a responsive pleading is allowed. If a pleading sets out a claim for relief that does not require a responsive pleading, an opposing party may assert at trial any defense to that claim. No defense or objection is waived by joining it with one or more other defenses or objections in a responsive pleading or in a motion.","line":43,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-6-2-1-09e4f75f","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":6,"target_pdf_page":39,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"6","text":"Coalition for Underground Expansion v. Mineta, 333 F.3d 193, 198 (D.C. Cir. 2003)","n":"1","kind":"case","target_doc":"2003 - Coalition for Underground Expansion v. Mineta, 333 F.3d 193","target_page":"198","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"at 198","status":"verified","target_quote":"District Court may in appropriate cases dispose of a motion to dismiss for lack of … subject matter jurisdiction under Fed. R.Civ.P. 12(b)(1) on the complaint standing alone,\" \"where necessary, the court may consider the complaint supplemented by undisputed facts evidenced in the record, or the complaint supplemented by undisputed facts plus the court's resolution of disputed facts.\" Herbert v. Nat'l Acad. of Scis., 974 F.2d 192, 197 (D.C.Cir.1992)","line":44,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-6-1-1-b97c0b09","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":6,"target_pdf_page":6,"target_pdf_page_end":null,"target_known":true,"target_pin_page":null,"target_columns":[6]},{"src_doc":"DDC-072","src_page":"6","text":"ECF No. 69 at 4","n":"1","kind":"ecf","target_doc":"DDC-069","target_page":"12","target_page_end":"","target_pin":"at 4 (printed)","status":"verified","target_quote":"","line":45,"k":1,"unit_id":"DDC-072-6-1-1-e80284a6","violations":[],"warnings":[],"src_pdf_page":6,"targe